From f23b04228f7e37227bb59323ebc9f0e189242327 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shammah Chancellor <shammah.chancellor@notvalid.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 03:03:35 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] net: reload outbound WebSocket TLS client certificates

Outbound wss handshakes previously reused the client certificate loaded at
startup even after the listener had adopted a rotated certificate. Check
certificate/key mtimes on new outbound TLS handshakes at the listener's
one-second cadence. Publish a fresh immutable client context only after the
certificate chain and matching key load successfully; keep the last good
identity on failure. Existing SSL sessions retain their original contexts.
Anonymous clients, peer SPKI pinning, inbound TLS and plain TCP are unchanged.

Add context tests for rotation, mismatched/malformed/missing files, zero
mtimes and retained SSL identities. A three-node functional test observes
the rotated SPKI on the next dial, keeps the first connection alive, and
checks mismatch fallback and recovery after fixing the key.

Validation: macOS arm64, Release, Chronik OFF and wallet OFF. All 17
net_websocket_tests cases (1,175 assertions), the new client-reload test,
and existing inbound certificate, peer-key and pinning functional tests
pass. Rebuilding the original startup-only dialer makes the new functional
fail at its first rotated-SPKI assertion. Linux, Windows, sanitizers and
the full functional suite were not run; no performance claim is made.
